"""English — details, curated_1 (61 questions)."""

D = {
"prophets_001": "In Surah Hud, Nuh is told that no more of his people will believe (11:36), and then receives the order: \"And build the ship under Our eyes and Our inspiration, and do not address Me concerning those who have wronged; indeed, they are to be drowned\" (11:37). As he built it, the chiefs of his people passed by and mocked him, and he answered that they would be mocked in turn (11:38). A Muslim learns from Nuh to keep at what Allah has commanded even while others laugh.",
"prophets_002": "In Surah Al-Anbiya, Ibrahim has broken his people's idols and shown them that idols cannot even speak (21:58-65). Their answer is, \"Burn him and support your gods\" (21:68). Then Allah speaks: \"O fire, be coolness and safety upon Ibrahim\" (21:69), and the next verse adds, \"They intended for him harm, but We made them the greatest losers\" (21:70). A Muslim learns that the fire obeys its Creator, and that whoever stands for Allah's oneness is in His care.",
"prophets_003": "Surah As-Saffat tells that Yunus fled to the laden ship, drew lots and lost (37:140-141); \"then the fish swallowed him while he was blameworthy\" (37:142). It adds: \"Had he not been of those who glorify Allah, he would have remained in its belly until the Day they are resurrected\" (37:143-144). Allah cast him onto the bare shore, ill, grew a gourd plant over him, and sent him back to his people, who believed (37:145-148). A Muslim learns that glorifying Allah in the darkest place is what brings the way out.",
"prophets_004": "Surah Yusuf records the king's words: \"I have seen seven fat cows being eaten by seven lean ones, and seven green ears of grain and others dry\" (12:43). His courtiers called it a jumble of dreams, but the cupbearer remembered Yusuf, still in prison, and went to ask him (12:44-46). Yusuf explained: seven years of sowing, seven hard years that would eat what was stored, then a year of rain (12:47-49). A Muslim learns that knowledge given by Allah is used to help people, even those who wronged us.",
"prophets_005": "Surah An-Nisa singles out one messenger: \"And messengers We have told you about before, and messengers We have not told you about; and Allah spoke to Musa directly\" (4:164). Surah Ta-Ha shows the scene in the sacred valley of Tuwa: \"Indeed, I am your Lord, so remove your sandals\" (20:12), and, \"Indeed, I am Allah; there is no god but Me, so worship Me and establish prayer for My remembrance\" (20:14). A Muslim learns that Allah honoured Musa with His speech, and that its first message was worship and prayer.",
"prophets_006": "The verse opens the story of Sulaiman in Surah An-Naml: \"And Sulaiman inherited Dawud. He said, 'O people, we have been taught the speech of birds, and we have been given from all things. Indeed, this is clear bounty'\" (27:16). The surah then shows him hearing an ant warn its colony and thanking Allah for His favour (27:18-19), and questioning the hoopoe that brought news of Saba (27:20-22). A Muslim learns that Sulaiman called his gift a bounty from Allah, and met it with thanks rather than pride.",
"prophets_007": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Isra: \"And your Lord is most knowing of whoever is in the heavens and the earth. And We have made some of the prophets exceed others, and to Dawud We gave the Zabur\" (17:55). Surah An-Nisa repeats it (4:163), and Surah Al-Anbiya quotes from it: \"We have written in the Zabur, after the reminder, that the earth will be inherited by My righteous servants\" (21:105). A Muslim believes in the Zabur as a Book of Allah given to a prophet who was also a king.",
"prophets_008": "In Surah Al 'Imran the angels tell Maryam of a son, the Messiah 'Isa (3:45). She asks how she can have a child when no man has touched her, and the answer comes: \"Such is Allah; He creates what He wills. When He decrees a matter, He only says to it, 'Be,' and it is\" (3:47). Later the surah compares him to Adam, created from dust by the same word, \"Be\" (3:59). A Muslim learns that 'Isa's birth was a sign of Allah's power, not a sign of divinity.",
"prophets_009": "The surah takes its name from the verse that opens her story: \"And mention in the Book Maryam, when she withdrew from her family to a place toward the east\" (19:16). The angel came to her as a man and said, \"I am only the messenger of your Lord to give you a pure boy\" (19:19); when she asked how, he answered that her Lord had said it was easy for Him (19:21). A Muslim honours Maryam as a woman the Qur'an names and praises.",
"prophets_010": "Surah Al-Anbiya records his prayer: \"And Ayyub, when he called to his Lord, 'Indeed, adversity has touched me, and You are the most merciful of the merciful.' So We responded to him and removed what afflicted him\" (21:83-84). The verse adds that his family was restored to him, as mercy from Allah (21:84). Surah Sad gives the verdict: \"We found him patient, an excellent servant; he was ever turning back\" (38:44). A Muslim learns that patience is not silence before Allah; Ayyub complained to Him alone, and was healed.",
"prophets_011": "The verse follows Allah's announcement to the angels of a successor on the earth, and the names that Adam knew and they did not (2:30-33). Then: \"And when We said to the angels, 'Prostrate before Adam,' so they prostrated, except Iblis. He refused and was arrogant and became of the disbelievers\" (2:34). Surah Al-A'raf records his excuse: \"I am better than him; You created me from fire and created him from clay\" (7:12). A Muslim learns that Adam was honoured by Allah's command, and that arrogance was the first sin.",
"prophets_012": "The title closes a verse of Surah An-Nisa: \"And who is better in religion than one who submits himself to Allah, does good, and follows the way of Ibrahim, inclining toward truth? And Allah took Ibrahim as a close friend\" (4:125). Surah An-Nahl describes the friend: \"Ibrahim was a nation in himself, devoutly obedient to Allah, inclining toward truth, and he was not of the polytheists\" (16:120). A Muslim learns that closeness to Allah is reached by submission and doing good, the two things the verse names before the friendship.",
"prophets_013": "Surah Yusuf tells that his brothers, jealous of their father's love for him, spoke of killing him, until one proposed the well so that a caravan would pick him up (12:8-10). \"So when they took him and agreed to put him into the bottom of the well, We inspired to him: you will surely tell them of this deed of theirs while they do not perceive\" (12:15). A Muslim learns that Allah was with Yusuf in the well, and kept the promise made there.",
"prophets_014": "Surah Hud tells the flood from the ark's building (11:37) to its end. When the oven gushed, Nuh loaded two of every kind and those who believed, \"and none had believed with him except a few\" (11:40). The ark sailed through waves like mountains; Nuh's son refused to board, chose a mountain and drowned (11:42-43). Then: \"O earth, swallow your water, and O sky, withhold\", and the ark settled on al-Judiyy (11:44). A Muslim learns that safety came from obeying Allah, not from family ties or high ground.",
"prophets_015": "Surah Ash-Shu'ara tells that Pharaoh's army pursued until the two groups saw each other, and Musa's companions cried, \"We are surely overtaken\" (26:61). He answered, \"No! Indeed, with me is my Lord; He will guide me\" (26:62). \"Then We inspired to Musa: strike the sea with your staff, and it parted, and each part was like a great towering mountain\" (26:63). Allah saved Musa and those with him (26:65). A Muslim learns from Musa's answer that certainty in Allah stands even with the sea ahead and the enemy behind.",
"prophets_016": "Surah Al-Anbiya tells the case: \"And Dawud and Sulaiman, when they judged concerning the field, when the sheep of a people had strayed into it at night, and We were witness to their judgement. And We gave understanding of it to Sulaiman, and to each We gave judgement and knowledge\" (21:78-79). The same verse adds that the mountains and the birds glorified Allah with Dawud (21:79). A Muslim learns that both prophets were honoured, and that sound judgement is a gift from Allah, not a matter of age.",
"prophets_017": "Surah Ta-Ha recalls: \"When We inspired to your mother what We inspired: cast him into the chest and cast it into the river, and the river will throw it onto the bank; there an enemy of Mine and an enemy of his will take him\" (20:38-39). Allah adds, \"I bestowed upon you love from Me\" (20:39); he was returned to his mother so that her eye would be comforted (20:40). A Muslim learns that Allah's plan ran through Pharaoh's house, and that a mother's obedience was rewarded.",
"prophets_018": "In Surah Al-A'raf, Salih calls Thamud to worship Allah alone and shows them the sign: \"This is the she-camel of Allah, a sign for you. So leave her to eat in Allah's land and do not touch her with harm, lest a painful punishment seize you\" (7:73). They hamstrung her and defied him to bring what he threatened (7:77), and the earthquake seized them in their homes (7:78). A Muslim learns that a sign from Allah is to be respected, and that mocking it brings its own end.",
"sira_001": "The Qur'an names the city where the Prophet ﷺ was born: it speaks of \"the valley of Makkah\" (48:24) and calls it \"the Mother of Cities\" (6:92). Surah Al-Balad swears by it: \"I swear by this city\" (90:1). Makkah is the town of the Ka'ba, the House that Ibrahim and Isma'il raised (2:127), and the Prophet ﷺ was born there among Quraysh, the tribe that kept it. A Muslim learns that the last prophet was born beside the first House built for the worship of Allah.",
"sira_002": "Surah Al-Fil asks the Prophet ﷺ: \"Have you not seen how your Lord dealt with the companions of the elephant? He sent against them birds in flocks, striking them with stones of baked clay, and made them like straw eaten up\" (105:1-5). The surah that follows draws the lesson for Makkah: \"Let them worship the Lord of this House, who has fed them against hunger and made them safe from fear\" (106:3-4). A Muslim learns that his birth year was marked by Allah's protection of His House.",
"sira_003": "Khadijah was a merchant of Makkah whose goods the Prophet ﷺ had carried in trade before she proposed marriage to him. When he came down from the cave of Hira trembling after the first revelation, he said to her, \"Cover me\", and she answered that Allah would never disgrace him, because he kept the ties of kinship, bore people's burdens, gave to the poor and honoured the guest. A Muslim learns that the first believer was a wife who knew the character of the man she believed in.",
"sira_004": "Before his mission, the Prophet ﷺ used to withdraw to the cave of Hira for nights of worship. There the angel came and said, \"Read\", and he answered that he could not read; the angel pressed him three times, then recited: \"Read in the name of your Lord who created. Read, and your Lord is the most Generous, who taught by the pen, taught man what he did not know\" (96:1-5). A Muslim learns that the first word of the revelation was a command to read.",
"sira_005": "Surah Al-Baqarah answers those who took Jibril as an enemy: \"Say: whoever is an enemy to Jibril, it is he who brought it down upon your heart by permission of Allah, confirming what came before it, and as guidance and good tidings for the believers\" (2:97). Surah Ash-Shu'ara calls him \"the Trustworthy Spirit\", who brought the Qur'an down upon the Prophet's ﷺ heart in clear Arabic (26:193-195). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an came by a trusted messenger from Allah to the heart of the Prophet ﷺ.",
"sira_006": "The word hijrah means leaving one's home for another, and the Qur'an names the emigrants from it: \"the first forerunners among the Muhajirun and the Ansar\" (9:100), those who left and those who took them in. Surah Al-Hashr calls them \"the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes and their properties, seeking bounty from Allah and His approval\" (59:8), and praises the people of Madinah who loved whoever emigrated to them (59:9). A Muslim dates the years from the Hijrah because that departure founded the community.",
"sira_007": "The Qur'an uses both names of the city. It quotes the hypocrites at the Trench: \"O people of Yathrib, there is no place for you here, so return\" (33:13), and elsewhere it speaks of \"the people of al-Madinah and the bedouins around them\" (9:120). Al-Madinah means \"the city\": it became the Prophet's ﷺ city, where its people, the Ansar, sheltered and aided those who emigrated (8:72). A Muslim learns that Madinah welcomed the Prophet ﷺ when Makkah drove him out, and holds his grave.",
"sira_008": "The verse is in Surah At-Tawbah: \"If you do not aid him, Allah has already aided him when those who disbelieved drove him out, the second of two, when they were in the cave, and he said to his companion, 'Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us'\" (9:40). The Qur'an leaves the companion unnamed, but every account of the Hijrah names Abu Bakr, who hid with the Prophet ﷺ while the pursuers searched. A Muslim learns that the calm of the cave came from trust in Allah.",
"sira_009": "Surah Al 'Imran reminds the believers: \"Allah had already given you victory at Badr while you were few; so fear Allah, that you may be grateful\" (3:123). The next verses recall the Prophet ﷺ promising them three thousand angels, and five thousand if they held firm (3:124-125), then: \"Allah made it only as good tidings for you; and victory is not except from Allah\" (3:126). A Muslim learns that Badr is remembered not for numbers, but for the help that came from Allah to a few who stood firm.",
"sira_010": "Surah Al-Fath had promised it: \"You will surely enter the Sacred Mosque, if Allah wills, in safety, with heads shaved and hair shortened, not fearing\" (48:27). Entering Makkah, the Prophet ﷺ rode with his head bowed low in humility, went to the Ka'ba and cast down the idols around it reciting, \"Truth has come and falsehood has departed\" (17:81), and pardoned the people who had driven him out. A Muslim learns that the greatest victory was met with humility and forgiveness.",
"sira_011": "The Farewell Sermon was delivered at 'Arafat during the Prophet's ﷺ only hajj. He told the people that their blood and property were as sacred to one another as that day; he charged them to treat women well and to hold to the Book of Allah. On that day was revealed: \"This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed My favour upon you\" (5:3). A Muslim learns that the Prophet's ﷺ last teaching was about the rights people owe each other.",
"sira_012": "The verse belongs to the passage of Surah Al-Baqarah on fasting: \"The month of Ramadan, in which the Qur'an was sent down. So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it\" (2:185). Surah Al-Qadr names the night within it: \"Indeed, We sent it down in the Night of Decree\" (97:1), and Surah Ad-Dukhan calls it \"a blessed night\" (44:3). A Muslim learns that the fast of Ramadan is tied to the Qur'an: the month of the Book is honoured by the fast.",
"sira_013": "Before revelation the Prophet ﷺ travelled with trade caravans, carrying the goods of Khadijah to Syria, and his honesty earned him the name al-Amin, the trustworthy. The Qur'an records his opponents' objection, \"What is this messenger who eats food and walks in the markets?\" (25:7), and answers that every messenger before him ate food and walked in the markets (25:20). A Muslim learns that honest trade was the daily work of the Prophet ﷺ, and that a believer's truthfulness is tested in the marketplace.",
"sira_014": "The Prophet's ﷺ father, 'Abdullah, died before he was born, and his mother, Aminah, while he was still a small child, so his grandfather 'Abd al-Muttalib took him into his care and kept the boy beside him at his seat by the Ka'ba. When the grandfather died, the care passed to his son Abu Talib. Surah Ad-Duha recalls this time: \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you shelter?\" (93:6). A Muslim learns that Allah watched over the orphan of Makkah through the hands of his family.",
"sira_015": "Abu Talib was the brother of the Prophet's ﷺ father, 'Abdullah, and the father of 'Ali. He took his nephew into his household after 'Abd al-Muttalib died, brought him on a trade journey to Syria, and after the revelation began he stood between him and Quraysh. He died in Makkah in the same year as Khadijah. The Qur'an says of those years, \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you shelter?\" (93:6). A Muslim learns that Allah made a loving uncle the shield of His Messenger.",
"sira_016": "Surah Al-Isra opens with the journey: \"Exalted is He who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, to show him of Our signs\" (17:1). Isra means a journey by night. Surah An-Najm tells of what followed, when the Prophet ﷺ saw the angel again \"at the Lote Tree of the utmost boundary\" (53:13-14) and \"saw of the greatest signs of his Lord\" (53:18). A Muslim learns that Makkah and Jerusalem are joined in one night's journey.",
"sira_017": "Fatimah was a daughter of the Prophet ﷺ and Khadijah, and 'Ali, son of Abu Talib, was his cousin, raised in his own household. Their marriage took place in Madinah, and from it came al-Hasan and al-Husayn, his grandsons. He said of her, in Sahih al-Bukhari, \"Fatimah is a part of me; whoever angers her angers me.\" She outlived him by a few months. A Muslim honours Fatimah as the daughter closest to the Prophet ﷺ, and her home as the one in which his grandsons grew up.",
"sira_018": "Surah At-Tawbah sets the year: \"Indeed, the number of months with Allah is twelve months in the register of Allah; of them, four are sacred\" (9:36). Muharram, whose name means \"the sacred one\", is one of those four and the first month; its tenth day, 'Ashura, is a day of fasting. The Muslims counted their years from the Hijrah, so the year begins with Muharram and the era with the migration to Madinah. A Muslim learns to see the calendar as a reminder of the Hijrah.",
"quran_001": "The surah is seven verses. It begins, \"In the name of Allah, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful. All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds\" (1:1-2), and ends with the request, \"Guide us to the straight path\" (1:6-7). The Qur'an refers to it in Surah Al-Hijr as \"seven of the oft-repeated\" (15:87), and the Prophet ﷺ called it the Mother of the Qur'an. A Muslim learns Al-Fatiha first of all, because every prayer is built on it: praise, then a plea for guidance.",
"quran_002": "The Qur'an opens with Al-Fatiha and closes with An-Nas, \"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind\" (114:1); between them stand 112 more, 114 in all. They are not arranged by order of revelation: the first verses revealed stand in the 96th surah, Al-'Alaq. Every surah begins with \"In the name of Allah\" except At-Tawbah, the ninth, and the whole is divided into thirty parts for reading in a month. A Muslim learns the count as the frame of the Book he will spend a lifetime reading.",
"quran_003": "Al-Baqarah, \"The Cow\", takes its name from the cow that the Children of Israel were told to sacrifice (2:67-71). It holds the longest verse of the Qur'an, on writing down a debt (2:282), and the Throne Verse, \"Allah, there is no god but Him\" (2:255). It ends with a prayer the believers still recite: \"Our Lord, do not burden us with what we have no strength to bear\" (2:286). A Muslim learns that the longest surah is a full course in the religion: belief, law and prayer together.",
"quran_004": "Three verses make up the surah: \"Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar. So pray to your Lord and sacrifice. Indeed, your enemy is the one cut off\" (108:1-3). Al-Kawthar means great abundance: the gift comes first, then the response it asks for, prayer and sacrifice to Allah alone, then the reply to his enemy. A Muslim learns that the shortest surah holds the whole pattern of gratitude: Allah gives, and the servant answers with worship.",
"quran_005": "Surah Yusuf opens with the verses of the clear Book (12:1) and then states it: \"Indeed, We have sent it down as an Arabic Qur'an so that you may understand\" (12:2). The Qur'an repeats the point: it came \"in a clear Arabic tongue\" (26:195), and had it been foreign its hearers would have asked why its verses were not made plain (41:44). A Muslim learns that the words of the Qur'an are Arabic, and reads a translation as its meaning, not as the Qur'an itself.",
"quran_006": "Surah Maryam opens with five letters, \"Kaf, Ha, Ya, 'Ayn, Sad\" (19:1), tells of Zakariyya, given the son Yahya (19:2-11), then turns to Maryam: \"And mention in the Book Maryam, when she withdrew from her family to a place toward the east\" (19:16). Her infant son speaks his first words in the surah: \"Indeed, I am the servant of Allah\" (19:30). A Muslim learns that the surah named after a mother is a surah about Allah's mercy to those who call on Him.",
"quran_007": "The Qur'an uses the word of its own chapters. It challenges its doubters, \"then produce a surah like it\" (2:23), and introduces Surah An-Nur with, \"A surah which We have sent down and made obligatory\" (24:1). Each surah bears a name taken from a word or story inside it; the longest has 286 verses and the shortest three. All but one begin with \"In the name of Allah.\" A Muslim learns to find his way in the Book by the names of its surahs.",
"quran_008": "The word ayah means a sign, and the Qur'an uses it for its verses and for the signs in creation. Surah Yusuf begins, \"These are the verses of the clear Book\" (12:1), while Surah Al-Baqarah says that in the heavens and the earth, night and day \"are signs for a people who use reason\" (2:164). Surah Fussilat: \"We will show them Our signs in the horizons and within themselves\" (41:53). A Muslim learns that reading a verse and looking at the sky are one act: reading the signs of Allah.",
"quran_009": "The hadith is narrated by 'Ubadah ibn as-Samit in Sahih al-Bukhari 756: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"There is no prayer for the one who does not recite the Opening of the Book.\" The Opening of the Book is Al-Fatiha, the seven verses the Qur'an calls \"seven of the oft-repeated\" (15:87). It is recited in every rak'ah, before any other surah, and it closes with the prayer for guidance to the straight path (1:6-7). A Muslim learns Al-Fatiha by heart, because no rak'ah stands without it.",
"quran_010": "The Qur'an names the one it was sent to: \"Muhammad is not the father of any of your men, but the Messenger of Allah and the seal of the prophets\" (33:40). It came down upon his heart by the Trustworthy Spirit, in clear Arabic (26:192-195), and in parts, \"that you may recite it to the people over a prolonged period\" (17:106). A Muslim learns that the Book and the Messenger are received together: the Qur'an is read as it was given to him.",
"faith_001": "The hadith is narrated by Ibn 'Umar in Sahih al-Bukhari 8: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"Islam is built on five: the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, establishing the prayer, giving the zakat, the hajj, and fasting Ramadan.\" The picture is a building on five supports, the testimony named first because the other four stand on it. A Muslim learns the five by name as the frame of the whole religion.",
"faith_002": "Sahih al-Bukhari 8, narrated by Ibn 'Umar, names it first of the five: \"the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.\" Both halves are in the Qur'an: \"So know that there is no god but Allah\" (47:19) and \"Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah\" (48:29). The same words ring out in every adhan. A Muslim learns that the religion opens with a witness spoken by the tongue and held in the heart.",
"faith_003": "Sahih al-Bukhari 8 lists \"establishing the prayer\" as the second of the five on which Islam is built. The Qur'an says that \"prayer has been decreed upon the believers at fixed times\" (4:103), and marks them: \"Establish prayer from the decline of the sun until the darkness of the night, and the recitation of dawn\" (17:78). The five are Fajr, Dhuhr, 'Asr, Maghrib and 'Isha. A Muslim learns that the day is shaped by five appointments with Allah, from before sunrise to after dark.",
"faith_004": "Sahih al-Bukhari 8 places \"giving the zakat\" third among the five. The Qur'an joins the two: \"Establish prayer and give zakat\" (2:43). It tells the Prophet ﷺ, \"Take from their wealth a charity by which you purify them and cause them increase\" (9:103), and the word zakat itself carries both meanings, purity and growth. Surah At-Tawbah names those who receive it (9:60). A Muslim learns that a share of his wealth belongs to others by right, and that giving it cleans what he keeps.",
"faith_005": "The command is in Surah Al-Baqarah: \"O you who believe, fasting has been decreed upon you as it was decreed upon those before you, so that you may become mindful of Allah\" (2:183). The next verse says the days are numbered, and lets the ill and the traveller make them up later (2:184); then the month is named: \"The month of Ramadan, in which the Qur'an was sent down\" (2:185). A Muslim learns that the fast is old, and that its purpose is a heart mindful of Allah.",
"faith_006": "The duty is stated in Surah Al 'Imran, after the Ka'ba is called the first House established for mankind (3:96): \"And due to Allah from the people is a pilgrimage to the House, for whoever is able to find a way\" (3:97). Surah Al-Hajj tells how the call began, when Allah ordered Ibrahim to proclaim the hajj so that people would come from far (22:27). A Muslim learns that the hajj is owed to Allah by whoever can reach His House, answering a call first given to Ibrahim.",
"faith_007": "Surah Al-Baqarah records the change of direction: \"We have certainly seen the turning of your face toward the heaven. So turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque; and wherever you are, turn your faces toward it\" (2:144). The verses before it foresee the foolish asking what turned the believers from their former qiblah, and answer that to Allah belong the east and the west (2:142). A Muslim learns that facing the Ka'ba is obedience to a verse, and that every mosque turns its rows toward one House.",
"faith_008": "Sahih Muslim 8 is the hadith of Jibril, narrated by 'Umar ibn al-Khattab. A man in white clothes and black hair sat before the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about Islam, then iman. The answer was: \"To believe in Allah, His angels, His books, His messengers, the Last Day, and to believe in the decree, its good and its bad.\" Afterwards the Prophet ﷺ said, \"That was Jibril; he came to teach you your religion.\" A Muslim learns the six as faith, taught by a question.",
"virtues_001": "The hadith is narrated by Abu Hurayrah in Sahih al-Bukhari 6018. It ties three duties to belief in Allah and the Last Day: the believer does not harm his neighbour, honours his guest, and \"speaks good or keeps silent.\" Every word will be answered for: \"Not a word does he utter except that with him is an observer prepared\" (50:18). A Muslim learns to weigh a sentence before speaking it, and to count silence as a good deed when nothing good is there to say.",
"virtues_002": "Sahih al-Bukhari 6018, narrated by Abu Hurayrah, binds the neighbour's rights to faith itself: whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day does no harm to his neighbour. The Qur'an sets the neighbour in the same list as parents and kin: \"Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, and to parents, relatives, orphans, the needy, the near neighbour, the neighbour farther away, and the companion at your side\" (4:36). Two kinds of neighbour are named. A Muslim learns that treating the neighbour well is part of believing.",
"virtues_003": "In Sahih al-Bukhari 6018 the Prophet ﷺ said that whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day should honour his guest. The Qur'an shows the model in Ibrahim: when \"the honoured guests\" came to him, he slipped away to his family, brought a fat calf, set it before them and said, \"Will you not eat?\" (51:24-27). He served them himself, quickly and unasked. A Muslim learns that a guest is an occasion for faith to show itself, and that welcoming him is a habit of the prophets.",
"virtues_004": "The hadith is narrated by Anas ibn Malik in Sahih al-Bukhari 13: the Prophet ﷺ said, \"None of you believes until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself.\" The Qur'an calls the believers brothers (49:10) and shows the love it means in the Ansar of Madinah, who \"prefer them over themselves, even though they are in need\" (59:9). A Muslim learns that faith is tested by what he wishes for other people, and lets that wish guide his hands.",
"virtues_005": "The hadith is narrated by 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr in Sahih al-Bukhari 10: the Prophet ﷺ said, \"The Muslim is the one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe, and the emigrant is the one who leaves what Allah has forbidden.\" Surah Al-Hujurat spells out what the tongue must not do: mock, insult, call people by hurtful names, suspect, spy or backbite (49:11-12). A Muslim learns that the name Muslim, from the root of peace and safety, is earned when others are safe from him.",
"virtues_006": "Surah Al-Isra sets kindness to parents after the worship of Allah: \"Your Lord has decreed that you worship none but Him, and to parents, good treatment. Say not to them 'uff', nor rebuke them, but speak to them a noble word\" (17:23). The next verse adds a prayer: \"Lower to them the wing of humility, and say: My Lord, have mercy upon them as they brought me up when I was small\" (17:24). A Muslim learns that even a sigh of impatience toward an ageing parent is too much.",
"prophets_019": "Surah Al-Baqarah tells how, after the announcement of a successor on the earth (2:30), \"He taught Adam the names; then He showed them to the angels and said, 'Inform Me of the names of these, if you are truthful'\" (2:31). The angels answered, \"Exalted are You; we have no knowledge except what You have taught us\" (2:32), and Adam told them the names (2:33). A Muslim learns that knowledge is a gift from Allah, that it raised Adam before the angels, and that the angels' answer is the honest one.",
}
